--TEST--
Spec § 7: `Core\Bytes::contains`, `startsWith` and `endsWith` search octets where `Core\Str`'s three search characters — they answer the same question on every needle a `string` can name, and 18 of the 28 windows of one subject are needles no `string` can name at all — `rule:types/bytes`, `rule:types/string-is-utf8` and `rule:types/conversion`, ADR 0063 R6
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `Core\Bytes` and `Core\Str` carry the same three predicate names, and every
// other case about them uses an ASCII subject, where the two classes cannot be
// told apart. `rule:types/string-is-utf8` makes a `string` position a *character* and § 1
// makes a `bytes` position an *octet*, so the whole difference lives on a
// subject whose characters are wider than one byte — and it is a difference in
// the **domain**, not in the answers: on a needle both classes can be asked,
// they agree, and what separates them is how many needles there are to ask
// about.
//
// The subject is 4 characters and 7 octets — `a` (1), `é` (2), `漢` (3), `b`
// (1) — so its interior boundaries are at octets 1, 3 and 6, and a window may
// start or end in three places no character does. `crates/nvs-stdlib/
// src/bytes.rs:608`, `:619` and `:629` are the three members; `str.rs:840`,
// `:850` and `:860` are their counterparts, and neither trio calls the other.

final class Unit {
    // `bool as string` renders `false` as the empty string (`rule:types/conversion`), so
    // a rendered answer needs a spelling of its own to be visible.
    public static function yn(bool $b): string {
        if ($b) { return "y"; }
        return "n";
    }

    // Whether a buffer is a needle `Core\Str` could be handed at all: a
    // `string` is UTF-8 by `rule:types/string-is-utf8`, so an octet run that splits a
    // character has no `string` spelling and no `Core\Str::contains` call.
    public static function namable(bytes $w): bool {
        return Core\Encoding::isValidText($w, Core\Charset::Utf8);
    }
}

string $subject = "aé漢b";
bytes $buffer = $subject as bytes;
int $chars = Core\Str::length($subject) as int;
int $octets = Core\Bytes::length($buffer) as int;

// 1. The two units, named on the same subject. `$subject as bytes` is the
//    identity on the buffer (`rule:types/conversion`), so the counts below are two
//    measurements of one value rather than of two.
echo "chars ", $chars, " octets ", $octets, " hex ", Core\Encoding::toHex($buffer), "\n";

// 2. Each character's own position, answered by both classes: `Core\Str`
//    counts characters and `Core\Bytes` counts octets, and the byte answer is
//    exactly the octet length of the character prefix before it. Counted, so a
//    member reporting the engine's byte offset through the `string` door — the
//    bug `rule:types/string-is-utf8` exists to prevent — fails here rather than looking
//    plausible on the `a` row, where the two units still coincide.
int $placed = 0;
int $differ = 0;
for (int $c = 0; $c < $chars; $c = $c + 1) {
    string $one = Core\Str::slice($subject, $c, 1);
    var $charAt = Core\Str::indexOf($subject, $one);
    var $byteAt = Core\Bytes::indexOf($buffer, $one as bytes);
    int $before = Core\Bytes::length(Core\Str::slice($subject, 0, $c) as bytes) as int;
    if ($charAt != null && $byteAt != null) {
        if (($charAt as int) == $c && ($byteAt as int) == $before) {
            $placed = $placed + 1;
        }
        if (($charAt as int) != ($byteAt as int)) {
            $differ = $differ + 1;
        }
    }
}
echo "placed ", $placed, "/", $chars, " differing offsets ", $differ, "\n";

// 3. Every character-aligned needle — the complete set of needles both classes
//    can be asked — put to all three predicates in both classes. 15 windows in
//    character space, three questions each, and the answers must be equal at
//    every one: the two trios are two searches over one buffer, and a `bytes`
//    predicate that decoded, or a `string` predicate that did not, disagrees
//    here while still printing plausibly on any single row.
int $aligned = 0;
int $agreed = 0;
int $held = 0;
for (int $start = 0; $start <= $chars; $start = $start + 1) {
    for (int $len = 0; $len <= $chars - $start; $len = $len + 1) {
        string $needle = Core\Str::slice($subject, $start, $len);
        bytes $raw = $needle as bytes;
        $aligned = $aligned + 1;
        if (Core\Str::contains($subject, $needle) == Core\Bytes::contains($buffer, $raw)) {
            $agreed = $agreed + 1;
        }
        if (Core\Str::startsWith($subject, $needle) == Core\Bytes::startsWith($buffer, $raw)) {
            $agreed = $agreed + 1;
        }
        if (Core\Str::endsWith($subject, $needle) == Core\Bytes::endsWith($buffer, $raw)) {
            $agreed = $agreed + 1;
        }
        // Not a tautology over three `false`s: every aligned window is a
        // substring of the subject, so all 15 are held by both classes.
        if (Core\Str::contains($subject, $needle) && Core\Bytes::contains($buffer, $raw)) {
            $held = $held + 1;
        }
    }
}
echo "aligned ", $aligned, " agreed ", $agreed, "/", $aligned * 3, " held ", $held, "\n";

// 4. Every non-empty window in *octet* space — the complete set of needles
//    `Core\Bytes` can be asked, which is what the class is for. All 28 are
//    contained, because the unit is the octet and nothing decodes; only 10 of
//    them are namable as a `string` at all, and those 10 are exactly the 10
//    non-empty windows the character sweep above produced. The other 18 split
//    a character at one end or the other, and `Core\Str::contains` cannot be
//    called with any of them.
//
// The empty window is deliberately outside this sweep and named on its own
// line below: it is aligned at every octet, so counting it here would put the
// same needle in the table eight times and blur exactly the 10/18 split this
// half exists to show.
int $windows = 0;
int $contained = 0;
int $namable = 0;
int $matched = 0;
for (int $from = 0; $from < $octets; $from = $from + 1) {
    for (int $width = 1; $width <= $octets - $from; $width = $width + 1) {
        bytes $window = Core\Bytes::slice($buffer, $from, $width);
        $windows = $windows + 1;
        if (Core\Bytes::contains($buffer, $window)) {
            $contained = $contained + 1;
        }
        if (Unit::namable($window)) {
            $namable = $namable + 1;
            // A namable window is one the character sweep already cut: search
            // the character-aligned needles for the same octets. `matched`
            // equalling `namable` is what makes "the two domains meet exactly
            // at the character boundaries" a count rather than a claim.
            bool $found = false;
            for (int $cs = 0; $cs <= $chars; $cs = $cs + 1) {
                for (int $cl = 1; $cl <= $chars - $cs; $cl = $cl + 1) {
                    if (Core\Bytes::compare(Core\Str::slice($subject, $cs, $cl) as bytes, $window) == 0) {
                        $found = true;
                    }
                }
            }
            if ($found) { $matched = $matched + 1; }
        }
    }
}
echo "windows ", $windows, " contained ", $contained, " namable ", $namable,
    " matched ", $matched, " unnamable ", $windows - $namable, "\n";

// 5. The three cuts a character has, named. A prefix ending inside `é`, a
//    suffix starting inside `漢`, and a window spanning the tail of one
//    character and the head of the next: each is found by the octet predicate
//    that owns that end, and none of the three is a `string`.
bytes $headSplit = Core\Encoding::fromHex("61c3");
bytes $tailSplit = Core\Encoding::fromHex("a262");
bytes $across = Core\Encoding::fromHex("a9e6");
echo "prefix ", Unit::yn(Core\Bytes::startsWith($buffer, $headSplit)),
    Unit::yn(Unit::namable($headSplit)),
    " suffix ", Unit::yn(Core\Bytes::endsWith($buffer, $tailSplit)),
    Unit::yn(Unit::namable($tailSplit)),
    " across ", Unit::yn(Core\Bytes::contains($buffer, $across)),
    Unit::yn(Unit::namable($across)), "\n";

// 6. The needle at the other end of the bound, where the two classes must not
//    diverge: the empty needle is a prefix, a suffix and a substring in both
//    classes, and a one-octet needle wider than nothing but narrower than a
//    character is held by `Core\Bytes` alone. The continuation octet of `é`
//    is that needle, and `Core\Str` has no spelling for it.
bytes $none = Core\Encoding::fromHex("");
bytes $continuation = Core\Encoding::fromHex("a9");
echo "empty ", Unit::yn(Core\Str::contains($subject, "")),
    Unit::yn(Core\Bytes::contains($buffer, $none)),
    Unit::yn(Core\Str::startsWith($subject, "")),
    Unit::yn(Core\Bytes::startsWith($buffer, $none)),
    Unit::yn(Core\Str::endsWith($subject, "")),
    Unit::yn(Core\Bytes::endsWith($buffer, $none)),
    " continuation ", Unit::yn(Core\Bytes::contains($buffer, $continuation)),
    Unit::yn(Unit::namable($continuation)), "\n";
--EXPECT--
chars 4 octets 7 hex 61c3a9e6bca262
placed 4/4 differing offsets 2
aligned 15 agreed 45/45 held 15
windows 28 contained 28 namable 10 matched 10 unnamable 18
prefix yn suffix yn across yn
empty yyyyyy continuation yn
